## When the spa town turns to gold

There are a few weeks each year when Mariánské Lázně looks exactly as a visitor dreams it should. The maples and beeches above the colonnade shift from summer green into ochre, copper and red. The morning mist lingers over the parks longer than in summer, and by midday a soft sun dissolves it — a sun that no longer burns, only warms. **Autumn in Mariánské Lázně** is not the fading end of the season; it is a quiet, distinct and remarkably rewarding chapter of the spa year.

While summer brings the crowds and the festivals, September and October offer what most guests are really longing for: calm, colour, fresh sub-montane air and spa treatments without the rush. If you are wondering when to set out for [a spa cure](/en/magazine/spa-treatment-guide), the golden season has several compelling arguments.

## A climate that heals even more in autumn

Mariánské Lázně sits at around 630 metres above sea level, ringed by the forests of the Slavkov Forest (Slavkovský les) Protected Landscape Area. Since 2023 the town has held the status of a climatic spa — and autumn is precisely when you see why. The local sub-montane climate is described as "toning": it gently stimulates and strengthens the body without exhausting it.

In autumn this quality comes fully into its own. The air is fresh, clear and scented with damp leaves and pine; the temperatures are pleasant for long walks, yet already cool enough to tone the body gently. For the respiratory system, circulation and quality of sleep, this season in a mountain spa town is ideal. It is no accident that climate therapy has always been one of the pillars of the local spa tradition.

## Springs without the queue

In high season you may occasionally have to wait at the best-known springs. In autumn you often have the colonnade almost to yourself. You can stroll unhurried from spring to spring, fill your spa cup and take advice on which source suits you — with no jostling and no rush.

Mariánské Lázně has around forty cold mineral springs within the town alone, and a hundred more in the wider surroundings. These are so-called acidulous springs, at a temperature of just 7 to 10 °C all year round — so whether you come in August or October, taste and effect stay the same. The Cross Spring and Ferdinand Spring traditionally accompany the drinking cure for metabolic complaints; the Rudolph Spring is associated with the kidneys and urinary tract, the Forest Spring with the respiratory system. Which water to drink, and how much, always belongs in the hands of a spa physician, however — the [drinking cure](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide) is a therapeutic treatment, not a drinks dispenser.

