## Forests full of mushrooms and colour

Autumn's greatest draw waits just beyond the town. The Slavkov Forest is one of the region's traditional mushrooming grounds — foresters and mycologists agree that the most mushrooms grow here in September, with the season running to the end of October and, for some species, into November. The region even has its own mycological club that advises foragers on what to pick and when. A morning outing into the woods with a basket, back for lunch, an afternoon wrap — that is an autumn spa day in its ideal form.

Even if you are not hunting mushrooms, the walk alone is worth it. The forest paths often lead straight to springs hidden among the trees, and in October they turn into tunnels of golden leaves. The silence of the forest and slow, mindful walking come close to the principle of forest bathing — and autumn is its most photogenic time.

## The autumn calendar: from folklore to apples

Anyone who imagines the spa town falls asleep at summer's end is mistaken. The autumn programme has its own, more intimate rhythm:

- **Marienbad Autumn (Mariánský podzim)** — an international folklore festival that, every year on the third September weekend, turns the town into a colourful backdrop of music and dance by Czech and foreign ensembles.
- **Spa Apple Festival** — a celebration of the autumn harvest right on the colonnade, with an exhibition of regional fruit varieties and garden produce. Early October here traditionally belongs to nature and good food.
- **Goethe Autumn** — a literary festival honouring the town's most famous guest, who returned to Mariánské Lázně again and again.

The August **Chopin Festival**, one of the oldest music festivals in the country, also echoes into autumn, so classical music hangs in the air long after the holidays. And do not forget: the **Singing Fountain** on the main colonnade plays until 31 October, when it bids the season a ceremonial farewell and falls silent until spring.

## An active autumn: golf, walking, cycling

The golden weather is an open invitation to move outdoors. The Royal Golf Club Mariánské Lázně — an 18-hole course founded in 1905 by King Edward VII — turns coppery in autumn and plays beautifully for as long as the Indian summer holds. Those who prefer a gentler pace will appreciate the network of promenades and waymarked routes for Nordic walking and cycling that run from the town into the surrounding forests and up to viewpoints.

Autumn exercise comes with a bonus, too: the cooler air makes longer efforts easier, and after a demanding day you savour the return to the warmth of the spa house all the more. The combination of an active day and an evening treatment is exactly what a spa stay is for.

## When to come — and why now

For a spa stay, autumn is something of an insider's tip. Prices and availability tend to be kinder once the summer peak has passed, the choice of hotels and treatments is wider, and staff have more time for their guests. Whether you long for a classic three-week cure or simply a long weekend to recharge, September and October offer a rare balance between a lively town and peaceful seclusion.

Mariánské Lázně is easy to reach, too — roughly 160 km from Prague and well connected by rail through the Czech and German networks. A few practical tips to close: pack layers and waterproof footwear for the forest paths, allow for shorter days and cool evenings, and book your treatments well ahead — even out of season, popular slots fill quickly. Whatever draws you here — the scent of leaves, a basket of mushrooms, or simply the wish to slow down — autumn in Mariánské Lázně gives you exactly what a spa has always been about: time for yourself.
